\b Role and task\b0
\par
You are a senior model analyst in the trading department of a major sportsbook, with 25 years' experience building and hardening tennis models for high ROI. Your job is to take existing models and turn them into robust, capital-ready systems: strong calibration, clear edge, and sustainable returns.
\par
I need you to \b use our current tennis fair-odds model and pipeline and transform it\b0  so it is robust and capable of high ROI.
\par

\b What we have (your starting point)\b0
\par
- Probabilistic model: serve/return, Elo, form, H2H, advanced stats, tier-aware calibration; outputs match win probabilities and fair odds.
\par
- Model version: \b Iteration 4 baseline (locked).\b0  Changes require explicit approval and backtest comparison. See docs/FAIR-ODDS-POLICY-LOCK.md for the full lock document.
\par
- Iteration 4 includes: tier-aware calibration/damping, ATP250 favorite guard, ATP500 hard calibration blend (conservative), series/confidence-specific delta multipliers, crisis downweight on rank in lower tiers.
\par
- Backtest vs Pinnacle closing (tennis-data, 2022-2025). Strict policy (Hard|Masters 1000, high confidence, value >= 10%) has shown ~+11-13% ROI in validation.
\par
- Live pipeline: daily fair odds, Pinnacle scrape, strict-policy signals, settlement (weekly), H2H/advanced-stats (weekly).
\par
- Codebase: scripts/backtest-fair-odds.py, scripts/oncourt-compute-fair-odds.py, and related scripts.
\par

\b Strict live policy (locked)\b0
\par
- API strict mode is ON.
\par
- Segment: Hard|Masters 1000.
\par
- Confidence: high.
\par
- Minimum value threshold: 10%.
\par
- This is the POLICY signal shown on the fair-odds page and used for strict reporting.
\par
- ATP500 hard short-favorite exclusion is wired (model favourite odds < 1.8, confidence = high) but only relevant if strict segments expand beyond Masters 1000.
\par

\b Tournament side-policy overlay (optional, off by default)\b0
\par
- Production mode supports base (strict only) or overlay (strict + tournament-segment filter). Default is base.
\par
- Enable with STRICT_POLICY_PRODUCTION_MODE=overlay plus overlay env vars (see FAIR-ODDS-POLICY-LOCK.md for full list).
\par
- Recommended: run strict report with overlay first for a few weeks; compare settled outcomes; enable in API only if evidence is good.
\par

\b Injury overlay (optional, off by default)\b0
\par
- Data: data/injured-players-tennisexplorer.csv from scripts/scrape-tennisexplorer-injured.py.
\par
- Strict/API: always computes injury flags (recent_injured_p1/p2/any). Optional filter overlay excludes candidates when a player is flagged.
\par
- Model: optional probability downweight (FAIR_ODDS_INJURY_DOWNWEIGHT_ENABLED, default false; delta 0.02, capped; cannot flip favourite).
\par
- Both overlays off in production until validation confirms improvement.
\par

\b Pinnacle scraper and daily pipeline\b0
\par
- Pinnacle odds are scraped via public guest API (guest.api.arcadia.pinnacle.com), men's singles only (ATP main/quals + Challenger), written to bookmaker_odds_snapshot in Supabase.
\par
- Daily pipeline: scripts/run-daily-odds.py runs at 23:55 UTC via scheduled task. It does: (1) Pinnacle scrape, (2) fair-odds compute, (3) strict policy report (appends signals to data/backtest/strict-signals.csv, plus overlay comparison in strict-signals-overlay-compare.csv).
\par
- The fair-odds API (src/app/api/fair-odds/route.ts) now falls back to yesterday's Pinnacle snapshot if today has no rows, so the page works between midnight UTC and the next scrape without errors.
\par

\b Settlement and weekly pipeline\b0
\par
- Settlement runs weekly (Sunday 22:00) via scripts/oncourt-weekly.ps1. Steps: OnCourt extract, Supabase sync, league averages, tournament averages, recent activity, Pinnacle scrape, fair-odds compute, strict report, settlement (scripts/settle-strict-signals.py on both base and overlay-compare CSVs), performance report (scripts/strict-policy-performance.py).
\par
- Pinnacle odds are the reference for measurement; 1 unit per bet (flat stakes).
\par
- Strict signals in data/backtest/strict-signals.csv are settled against actual match results; overlay comparison in strict-signals-overlay-compare.csv.
\par

\b Individual tip pages (SEO-indexed)\b0
\par
- Each bet/tip has its own page at /tips/[slugId] (e.g. /tips/altmaier-vs-kecmanovic-42).
\par
- Slug is auto-generated from event name + id (src/lib/slugify.ts). No DB column needed.
\par
- Page is server-rendered with full SEO metadata (unique title, description, canonical, OpenGraph). Revalidates every 60 seconds.
\par
- Hero layout: player name large and bold, selection as emerald subtitle (e.g. "Moneyline" not "ML"), match as context. 3-column card for odds, stake, bookmaker.
\par
- Old /tips/123 URLs redirect to the slug URL.
\par
- All tip listing pages (tennis-tips, player-props, homepage) link each event to its tip page.
\par
- Sitemap (src/app/sitemap.ts) is async and includes up to 500 tip URLs from Supabase so Google can discover and index them.
\par
- Google will index individual match tips (e.g. "Sakatsume vs Parks betting tip"). Long-tail match-specific queries are the main SEO target.
\par

